            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "cloud-top pressure",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "One of the unique features of CCFM is its ability to determine the distribution of cloud sizes and updraught velocities in a given grid-scale environment, making it suitable for the study of convective-cloud microphysics and aerosol effects as well as cloud-field morphology. Figure 11a shows the annual and global joint distribution of cloud-base radius and updraught velocity from the simulation using CCFM (L-2 configuration)...We can also obtain the joint distribution of the maximum radius reached by the updraught in each column, and the pressure at its cloud top, shown in Fig. 11b.",
